Jahshaka patch 0041 — a descriptor-set cache keyed on a raw pointer must not
outlive the pointee: the buffer creation serial, plus a dangling-pointer twin.

SOURCE patch. EVERY TREE MUST RE-RUN `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` after pulling
this (reset the ogre-next submodule first if it already carries an earlier
revision of any patch: `git -C irisgl/thirdparty/ogre-next checkout -- . &&
git -C irisgl/thirdparty/ogre-next clean -fd`).

THE DEFECT, AS THE USER MET IT (Jahshaka ledger §373, PHOTON_SPEC §7 E1 item 0).
On a cold shader cache, a global-illumination re-solve issued ONE rendered frame
after Ogre's inline (serial) shader compile baked a picture with no voxel bounce
in it at all — a material edit or a light drag in the first second of a scene,
and the scene stayed wrongly lit until something else re-solved. Reproduced 3/3
on an RTX 4080S: the same scene, same content, same everything, centre pixel
(54,36,1) with the parallel compile queue and (46,32,1) with the serial one; the
VctVoxelizer's albedo volume held 14,036 occupied voxels in the first and ZERO in
the second. The irradiance field, where the search started, was a victim: it cone
traces those voxels, so an empty volume bakes an empty field.

THE CAUSE, named by the driver. Under Vulkan core validation the failing half —
and only the failing half — raises

  VUID-vkCmdDispatch-format-07753: the uniform texel buffer descriptor
  [Set 1, Binding 0, variable "inMeshBuffer"] requires UINT component type, but
  bound descriptor format is VK_FORMAT_R32G32_SFLOAT.

`inMeshBuffer` is VCT/AabbCalculator's mesh table, a PFG_RGBA32_UINT TexBuffer
(VctVoxelizer::prepareAabbCalculatorMeshData). VK_FORMAT_R32G32_SFLOAT is
IrradianceField's integration-taps buffer (PFG_RG32_FLOAT,
IrradianceField::setupIntegrationTaps). The AABB job was reading the irradiance
field's weights as mesh records, so every mesh reported zero indices, the
parallel reduction never ran, every mesh AABB came back as the ±FLT_MAX sentinel
the shader initialises with, every instance AABB was degenerate and the
voxelization wrote nothing.

HOW THE TWO MET. Traced through HlmsManager, HlmsComputeJob, VulkanRootLayout and
VulkanDescriptorSetTexture2, in the failing run:

  create  partsub=0x5a10115dd6f0 vbo=14966944          <- new mesh table
  cacheT2 HIT set=... rsData=... slot0buf=0x5a10115dd6f0 refs=2
  rlBind  set=1 ... {b0 t4 n1 view=0x5a100fd3d020}     <- the WRONG view

and ~100 log lines earlier:

  vkSetCtor this=... view=0x5a100fd3d020 buf=0x5a10115dd6f0 fmt=13 vbo=11141552

fmt 13 is PFG_RG32_FLOAT. The irradiance field's taps buffer had lived at
0x5a10115dd6f0, was destroyed when the field was rebuilt, and the allocator handed
that address to the voxelizer's new mesh table. `HlmsManager::getDescriptorSetTexture2`
keys its cache on `DescriptorSetTexture2::BufferSlot`, whose identity is the raw
`BufferPacked *` plus offset and size — so the lookup HIT the dead buffer's entry
(still referenced by the field's own compute jobs, refs=1 before the hit) and
handed the AABB job the dead buffer's VkBufferView: wrong suballocation offset,
wrong format. The parallel half MISSED the same cache only because its allocation
history put the new buffer somewhere else. That is the whole dependence on the
shader-compile branch, and the whole reason it needs a cold cache.

NOT the site, ruled out by the same trace and reported here so nobody re-walks it:
HlmsComputeJob::setTexBuffer's "same pointer, same offset, same size = unchanged"
short circuit took the CHANGED branch (the cache lookup only happens because it
did), and VulkanRootLayout::bind reported firstDirtySet 0 and wrote the texel
buffer binding on every dispatch — it bound faithfully the view it was given.

THE FIX, AT THE CLASS. A pointer is not an identity for anything that outlives the
pointee, and the engine has no buffer-destroyed notification to hang invalidation
on. So BufferPacked gains a process-wide, monotonic, never-reused creation serial,
and the two descriptor BufferSlots carry it and compare it. A buffer created at a
dead one's address now compares DIFFERENT: the cache misses, a correct descriptor
set is built, and the slot comparison in HlmsComputeJob sees the change too. The
serial is a STORED field stamped by whoever fills the slot while the buffer is
alive, never read through the pointer inside the comparison — the comparison runs
against slots that may already name a destroyed buffer, and dereferencing one
would be the very use-after-free this prevents.

Call sites that assign `BufferSlot::buffer` directly leave the serial at 0 and keep
the pre-patch behaviour exactly: nothing regresses, they are simply not protected.
The two that matter — HlmsComputeJob::setTexBuffer and ::_setUavBuffer, which every
compute job and every compositor compute pass goes through — stamp it.

AND TWO MORE OF THE SAME BUG CLASS, found on the way and fixed here:

  * VulkanRenderSystem::_descriptorSetSamplerDestroyed wrote the address of the
    object it was about to delete back into mGlobalTable.bakedDescriptorSets
    [Samplers] instead of clearing it — a dangling pointer that _setSamplers then
    matches against a recycled allocation and skips the rebind. The compute twin
    eight lines below has always cleared it correctly; this is a copy-paste slip.

  * HlmsComputeJob::analyzeBarriers resolved no transition at all for a PLAIN
    TexBufferPacked read by a compute job: the existing branch only fires for a
    read-only VIEW of a UAV buffer. The solver therefore had no record of such a
    buffer and could not order a later write against the read. Registering it emits
    no barrier by itself (BarrierSolver::resolveTransition produces nothing for a
    first-seen resource) — it only lets the solver see a hazard if one appears.

WHAT IT IS NOT. It is not an invalidate-on-destroy notification from VaoManager to
HlmsManager. That was considered and rejected: erasing a cached descriptor set
whose buffer has died would dangle the pointers of every job still holding it
(the field's integration jobs hold exactly such references), and with the serial in
place the invalidation is not needed for correctness — a dead entry can never be
matched again. The absence of that notification remains an upstream issue: those
entries leak until their holders release them, which is what they did before.

VERIFIED. `git apply --check` and `--reverse --check` against the pristine pin with
0001-0040 applied. Repro, both compile branches, cold cache, virgin homes, 3/3:
both halves now (54,36,1), PNGs byte-identical. Under core validation
VUID-vkCmdDispatch-format-07753 is gone from both halves and the two pre-existing
VUIDs are unchanged in count. `--engine-selftest` sha256 unchanged.

